tion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504573 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504573 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c61d95f8-ddcc-5e61-a389-2bf5488d153d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504602 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504602 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504602 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504602 #edb/ref 1071 "The interval $x\\in (-14,\\infty )$ corresponds to the inequality" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504602 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05484b4a-57a2-55c2-9049-f423ce09e86a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504602 #edb/ref 1074 "The statement $x\\in (-14,\\infty )$ means that $x$ lies between $-14$ and $\\infty ,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-14$ is *not* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $\\infty $ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-14<x<\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are smaller than infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\n-14<x\n$$\n\nor, equivalently, \n\n$$\nx>-14.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504605 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504605 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504605 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504605 #edb/ref 1071 "The inequality $x<5$ can be expressed in interval notation as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504605 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b34c9dd-15c6-517b-a545-622b3797f82a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504605 #edb/ref 1074 "The inequality $x<5$ corresponds to all numbers less than $5.$ A number line for this interval is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/61/61a3fcdcc19f822c0d94b806441ea2888dab3bb9ac0b846c965a8fe23ad078ae.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- There is no left endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with negative infinity as the left endpoint, not included.\n\n- The right endpoint $5$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x<5$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,5).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504640 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504640 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504640 #edb/ref 1071 "The interval notation  $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7)$ corresponds to the inequality" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504640 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d843358-b3f1-509e-8689-eba95475d008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504640 #edb/ref 1074 "The statement $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7)$ means that $x$ lies between $-\\infty $ and $-7,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-\\infty $ is *not* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $-7$ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-\\infty <x<-7.\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are greater than negative infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\nx<-7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504642 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504642 #edb/ref 1069 "q-28812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504642 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504642 #edb/ref 1071 "The inequality $x<-2$ can be expressed in interval notation as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504642 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7495bbc7-a548-574a-82ac-70b0b5806589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504642 #edb/ref 1074 "The inequality $x<-2$ corresponds to all numbers less than $-2.$ A number line for this interval is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/d1/d142ec418d86ccfce8f701ca19384d599ed2b5f7072d5ae84dcb3796baa73261.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- There is no left endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with negative infinity as the left endpoint, not included.\n\n- The right endpoint $-2$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x<-2$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-2).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504649 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504649 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154802" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504649 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504649 #edb/ref 1071 "The inequality $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504649 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8301710f-dfbb-5680-866d-b67f486e14d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504649 #edb/ref 1074 "The inequality $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ corresponds to all numbers greater than $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d43b5c54e0beb341f682f44ee4c6d42821a93d45e7d5f0c5576c13e69bff903.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $z>-\\frac{1}{2}$ as\n\n$$\nz\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504660 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504660 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154778" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504660 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504660 #edb/ref 1071 "The inequality $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504660 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4783dad-574a-5127-8f16-79c4f5746713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504660 #edb/ref 1074 "The inequality $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ corresponds to all numbers greater than $\\frac{1}{2}.$ A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/12/122ab4938bf6102036dec1a704cca8afa344d6ca3b9558743c3d573d16367636.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* included in the interval.\n\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ as\n\n$$\nx\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504681 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504681 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504681 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504681 #edb/ref 1071 "The interval notation $z\\in (-\\infty ,26]$ corresponds to the inequality" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504681 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca3bed50-c15f-520c-b7d3-e53cd1fcae8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504681 #edb/ref 1074 "The statement $z\\in (-\\infty ,26]$ means that $z$ lies between $-\\infty $ and $26,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-\\infty $ is *not* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $26$ *is* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-\\infty <z\\le 26.\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are greater than negative infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\nz\\le 26.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504697 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504697 #edb/ref 1069 "q-154829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504697 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504697 #edb/ref 1071 "The interval $y\\in [-16,\\infty )$ corresponds to the inequality" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504697 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df8b3cd8-e582-5e68-8862-832cf8d8a7f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504697 #edb/ref 1074 "The statement $y\\in [-16,\\infty )$ means that $y$ lies between $-16$ and $\\infty ,$ where \n\n- the left endpoint $-16$ *is* included, and\n\n- the right endpoint $\\infty $ is *not* included.\n\nTherefore, the inequality represented by this interval is \n\n$$\n-16\\le y<\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, since all numbers are smaller than infinity, it suffices to write \n\n$$\n-16\\le y\n$$\n\nor, equivalently, \n\n$$\ny\\ge -16.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
